## What happened

Apple removed DSA support from its native Security framework (Common Crypto). .NET on macOS delegates to the platform crypto provider, so starting in .NET 11, `DSA.Create()` throws **`PlatformNotSupportedException`** on macOS — there is no underlying implementation to call.

Windows and Linux are unaffected because Windows CNG and OpenSSL still support DSA.

## How to fix it

**Option A (recommended) — Migrate off DSA entirely.**  
DSA is considered legacy/weak. If you only need to *verify* old signatures for a one-time migration, do so and then move to ECDSA or RSA-PSS going forward.

**Option B — Use OpenSSL explicitly on macOS.**  
Ship or reference OpenSSL (e.g. via Homebrew's `libssl`) and P/Invoke or use a library that wraps it. You can conditionally create the provider:

```csharp
using System.Runtime.InteropServices;
using System.Security.Cryptography;

DSA CreateDsa()
{
    if (RuntimeInformation.IsOSPlatform(OSPlatform.OSX))
    {
        // Use an OpenSSL-backed implementation (e.g. a third-party package
        // or a custom interop wrapper around libssl).
        return DSAOpenSsl.Create(); // hypothetical; see below
    }
    return DSA.Create();
}
```

In practice you'd reference a package like **Portable.BouncyCastle** to get a managed DSA implementation that works everywhere:

```csharp
// Using Bouncy Castle for cross-platform DSA verification
var dsaParams = /* import your legacy key */;
var verifier = SignerUtilities.GetSigner("SHA256withDSA");
verifier.Init(false, publicKey);
verifier.BlockUpdate(message, 0, message.Length);
bool valid = verifier.VerifySignature(signature);
```

**Option C — Target .NET 10 on macOS only.**  
Pin your macOS build to the last runtime that supported it (short-term workaround only).

---

**TL;DR:** Apple dropped DSA → .NET 11 can no longer provide it on macOS. Best fix: use Bouncy Castle for a portable managed implementation, or migrate to a modern algorithm.
